**What changed:** The `@angular/animations` package is deprecated as of v20.2. It is recommended to replace it with native CSS/JS libraries using the new `animate.enter` and `animate.leave` features, which significantly reduce JavaScript bundle size and improve performance by leveraging hardware acceleration. The `AnimationPlayer` class is also deprecated in favor of the native `Element.getAnimations()` and Web Animations API, allowing manual control of animations with methods like `cancel()`, `play()`, `pause()`, and `reverse()`. Animations can now be triggered by toggling CSS styles or classes, and sequencing is handled by CSS delays or `animationend` events. Unlike the previous package, animations in nested child component templates do not fire when a parent component removes an element or view.
(https://angular.dev/guide/animations/migration)
**Affects:** Applications using the `@angular/animations` package.
**Action:** required: refactor all animations based on `@angular/animations` to use native CSS or JS animation libraries with `animate.enter` and `animate.leave`. Remove dependencies on the `@angular/animations` package, including `provideAnimations` and `AnimationPlayer`, and replace them with native CSS class-based triggers and `Element.getAnimations()` for control.
**Before/after:**
```css
/* Animations can be triggered natively by adding a class.
   Removing the class reverts the element. This eliminates the
   need for the trigger() function from @angular/animations. */

.trigger-class {
  animation: my-animation 300ms;
}

@keyframes my-animation {
  /* keyframes */
}
```

**What changed:** `Router.getCurrentNavigation` method is deprecated in Angular 20.2.0 and replaced by `Router.currentNavigation` signal ((https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/refs/tags/20.2.0/CHANGELOG.md), (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/62971)). `Router.currentNavigation` is a `Signal<Navigation | null>` that holds the current `Navigation` object when the router is navigating, and `null` when idle ((https://angular.dev/api/router/Router)). The deprecated method also returned the `Navigation` object when the router was navigating and `null` when idle ((https://angular.dev/api/router/Router)).

**Affects:** Applications using `router.getCurrentNavigation()`

**Action:** required: Replace all usages of `Router.getCurrentNavigation` method with the `Router.currentNavigation` signal.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Before (deprecated in 20.2)
const currentNavigation = this.router.getCurrentNavigation();

// After (recommended)
const currentNavigation = this.router.currentNavigation();
```

**What changed:** A compiler fix in the template pipeline (`packages/compiler/src/template/pipeline/src/ingest.ts`, `ingestHostEvent`) now handles `ParsedEventType.Animation` host bindings by creating an `ir.createAnimationListenerOp` (with `ir.AnimationKind.ENTER`/`ir.AnimationKind.LEAVE`) instead of a generic `ir.createListenerOp`. Previously, host bindings/listeners for `(animate.enter)` and `(animate.leave)` (e.g. `host: {'(animate.enter)': 'fadeFn($event)'}` or `@HostListener`) were ingested incorrectly and never fired; they now emit `ɵɵanimateEnterListener(...)` / `ɵɵanimateLeaveListener(...)` in hostBindings alongside `ɵɵanimateEnter("slide")` / `ɵɵanimateLeave("slide")` on the element (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/7b517288139aec166e5e5b60e84b1e22e3d6b70f.patch, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/63217). Reported in issue #63199 as missing host support for animation lifecycle events on the host element (https://github.com/angular/angular/issues/63199).

**Affects:** Components/directives that declare host bindings or listeners for `(animate.enter)` / `(animate.leave)` (or bracket bindings like `host: {'[animate.leave]': 'fade()'}`) — these previously silently did nothing and now start firing their handlers with an `AnimationCallbackEvent`.

**Action:** none — no code, config, or dependency changes required; re-verify that animation host handlers (which used to be dead code) behave correctly now that they run, and that no logic was added as a workaround for them not firing.

**Before/after:**
```ts
@Component({
  selector: 'child-component',
  host: {'(animate.enter)': 'fadeFn($event)'},
  template: `<p>Sliding Content</p>`,
})
export class ChildComponent {
  fadeFn(event: AnimationCallbackEvent) {
    event.target.classList.add('fade');
    event.animationComplete();
  }
}
// Now compiles to host listener via ɵɵanimateEnterListener instead of a plain listener:
// i0.ɵɵanimateEnterListener(function ChildComponent_animateenter_HostBindingHandler($event) { return ctx.fadeFn($event); });
```

**What changed:** The extended template diagnostic NG8117 (`ErrorCode.UNINVOKED_FUNCTION_IN_TEXT_INTERPOLATION = 8117`, check name `uninvokedFunctionInTextInterpolation`) previously rendered the offending function name by slicing the raw template source with a helper `generateStringFromExpression(expression, source)` in `packages/compiler-cli/src/ngtsc/typecheck/extended/checks/uninvoked_function_in_text_interpolation/index.ts`; that produced garbled text such as `Function in text interpolation should be invoked: ect Object]()` because the span offsets did not line up with the interpolated source string. The helper and the `sourceSpan` / `AbsoluteSourceSpan` plumbing were removed and the message is now built from the AST node name: `` const errorString = `Function in text interpolation should be invoked: ${expression.name}()` ``, yielding e.g. `Function in text interpolation should be invoked: firstName()` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/c4917074f1e278ea24948a8810b3d4f306765174, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/62842). Only the message text changed; the check itself, its code, category and span of the reported diagnostic are unchanged (tests still assert `ts.DiagnosticCategory.Warning` and `getSourceCodeForDiagnostic(diags[0])` is `firstName`, now also `messageText` contains `firstName()`). NG8117 fires for templates like `<p> {{ firstName }} </p>` and only runs when extended diagnostics with `strictTemplates` are enabled (https://angular.dev/extended-diagnostics, https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/c4917074f1e278ea24948a8810b3d4f306765174/packages/compiler-cli/src/ngtsc/diagnostics/src/error_code.ts).

**Affects:** Applications with the NG8117 / `uninvokedFunctionInTextInterpolation` extended diagnostic firing, and any tooling, tests, lint baselines or log-parsing that string-matched the previously garbled message text.

**Action:** none — no code, configuration or dependency change is needed; only re-verify any test or tooling that asserts the exact NG8117 message text.

**Before/after:**
```diff
-      const fullExpressionText = generateStringFromExpression(expression, sourceSpan.toString());
-
-      const errorString = `Function in text interpolation should be invoked: ${fullExpressionText}()`;
+      const errorString = `Function in text interpolation should be invoked: ${expression.name}()`;
```

**What changed:** Angular 20.2 introduces new enter and leave animation instructions, implemented as directives/properties (`animate.enter` and `animate.leave`) and output events (`(animate.enter)` and `(animate.leave)`). These allow attaching CSS animation classes to elements when they are added to or removed from the DOM, or invoking custom JavaScript animation logic that uses Web Animations API or third-party libraries. The new syntax replaces the complex `@angular/animations` package for most enter/leave use cases, with `@angular/animations` being deprecated in this release (https://github.com/angular/angular/releases/tag/20.2.0, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/62682).

**Affects:** applications using animations on conditional elements (`@if`, `@for`), or those using `@angular/animations` (deprecated).

**Action:** none

**Before/after:**
```html
@if (display()) {
  <div animate.enter="fade-in" animate.leave="fade-out">Content</div>
}

@if (display()) {
  <div (animate.leave)="rotate($event)">Content</div>
}
```

**What changed:** Angular 20.2.0 introduces the `inferTagName` option in `TestBed.createComponent` and `TestBed.configureTestingModule` to align test behavior with the runtime. By default, `inferTagName` is `false`, meaning `TestBed` creates the component as a `div` regardless of the selector defined in `@Component`. In the runtime, the tag name is inferred from the first selector in `@Component`, falling back to `div` only if no selector is present. When `inferTagName` is set to `true` during testing, `TestBed` infers the tag name from the component's selector, ensuring the component behaves as it would in production (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/cec91c00356ee3974c39c9471b243a2a16149f5b, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/62283).

**Affects:** Applications using `TestBed.createComponent` or `TestBed.configureTestingModule` in unit tests where the component's selector differs from `div`, and where tests rely on tag-name-specific behavior or CSS selectors that might behave differently if the tag name is not inferred.

**Action:** required: Set `inferTagName` to `true` in `TestBed.configureTestingModule` or `TestBed.createComponent` options if you want to enable the new behavior. Note that this option is expected to default to `true` in a future version (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/cec91c00356ee3974c39c9471b243a2a16149f5b).

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Before (default behavior in 20.2.0, always uses 'div')
TestBed.configureTestingModule({
  declarations: [MyComponent]
});
// or
const fixture = TestBed.createComponent(MyComponent);

// After (opting into inferred tag names)
TestBed.configureTestingModule({
  declarations: [MyComponent],
  // Note: Documentation mentions it as an option, but specific placement in configureTestingModule vs createComponent options
  // needs verification. Commit message says "add the `inferTagName` option to `TestBed.createComponent` and `TestBed.configureTestingModule`"
  // Ninja Squad blog shows: TestBed.createComponent(..., { inferTagName: true })?
  // Actually, Ninja Squad says: "The TestBed.createComponent and TestBed.configureTestingModule methods have a new option inferTagName."
  // Since I don't have the exact syntax from the code, I will stick to the commit description logic.
});
```
