**Final Answer Construction:**
**What changed:** In Angular 20.2.0, the `inferTagName` option was added to `TestBed.createComponent` and `TestBed.configureTestingModule`. Previously, `TestBed` always created components as a `div` element during tests, whereas the runtime infers the tag name from the component's selector. This change allows tests to opt into inferring the tag name from the selector to match runtime behavior. The `inferTagName` option defaults to `false` but is planned to default to `true` in a future version (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/cec91c00356ee3974c39c9471b243a2a16149f5b, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/62283).
**Affects:** Tests using `TestBed.createComponent` or `TestBed.configureTestingModule` where the component selector results in a tag name other than `div`, and where the difference in tag name affects the test outcome or validity.
**Action:** required: Optionally set `inferTagName` to `true` in `TestBed.createComponent` or `TestBed.configureTestingModule` to align test behavior with the runtime. This behavior will likely become the default in a future major release (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/cec91c00356ee3974c39c9471b243a2a16149f5b).

**What changed:** The `provideZonelessChangeDetection` function in `@angular/core` moved from `@developerPreview 20.0` to `@publicApi 20.2`, promoting zoneless change detection to stable status for production use (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/62699/files, https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/20.2.0/packages/core/src/change_detection/scheduling/zoneless_scheduling_impl.ts). The roadmap now lists "Zoneless change detection" under "Production ready" (https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/20.2.0/adev/src/content/reference/roadmap.md).

**Affects:** Applications that currently enable zoneless via `provideZonelessChangeDetection()`, or applications considering adopting zoneless which previously avoided it due to developer preview API instability guarantees.

**Action:** required: re-verify zoneless change detection behaviour. Applications already using `provideZonelessChangeDetection()` require no code changes; the function signature and behavior remain the same. Applications adopting zoneless for the first time should follow the zoneless guide requirements: remove `zone.js` from `polyfills` in `angular.json` and from `package.json` dependencies, and ensure components use signal-based updates, `markForCheck`, or `OnPush` strategy to trigger change detection (https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/20.2.0/adev/src/content/guide/zoneless.md).

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Standalone bootstrap (unchanged, now stable)
bootstrapApplication(MyApp, {providers: [
  provideZonelessChangeDetection(),
]});

// NgModule bootstrap
@NgModule({
  providers: [provideZonelessChangeDetection()]
})
export class AppModule {}
```

**What changed:** Angular 20.2 expands the `as` alias keyword (previously only allowed on `@if`) to `@else if` blocks in the built-in control flow syntax, via commit a409534d6c / PR #63047 (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/a409534d6c3d7cb4472afffd6b17df8c25e34106, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/63047). An alias is written after the condition separated by a semicolon: `@else if (value(); as alias) { {{alias}} }`. Multiple `@else if` branches may each declare aliases (with different or the same names), and aliases work in nested `@else if` blocks with proper scoping; the compiler generates them via `ɵɵconditional` context assignment (e.g. `$tmp_1_0$ = ctx.three` reused across branches) (patch: https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/a409534d6c3d7cb4472afffd6b17df8c25e34106.patch). Compiler changes were in `packages/compiler/src/render3/r3_control_flow.ts` and `packages/compiler-cli/src/ngtsc/typecheck/... conditionals.ts`, with template type-checking support (`var _t1 = ...; else if (...) && _t1`) and Language Service quick-info updates.

**Affects:** Templates using `@if`/`@else if` blocks. Only purely additive: code that previously compiled is unchanged; `as` on `@else if` was a compile error before 20.2.

**Action:** none — this is an optional new template capability; no code, configuration, or dependency changes are required when upgrading.

**Before/after:**
```angular-html
<!-- Before 20.2: `as` allowed only on @if; using it on @else if was invalid -->
@if (users$ | async; as users) { {{ users.length }} }

<!-- Angular 20.2: `as` now also works on @else if (from commit test cases) -->
@if (one; as foo) {
  One: {{foo}}
} @else if (two) {
  Two: {{two}}
} @else if (three; as bar) {
  Three: {{bar}}
} @else if (value(); as alias) {
  {{value()}} as {{alias}}
}
```

**What changed:** Angular 20.2 added support for TypeScript 5.9 and widened the accepted peer-dependency range in `@angular/compiler-cli` from `typescript: ">=5.8 <5.9"` to `typescript: ">=5.8 <6.0"` (https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/20.2.x/packages/compiler-cli/package.json). The official compatibility table now lists `20.2.x || 20.3.x` as supporting TypeScript `>=5.8.0 <6.0`, while `20.0.x || 20.1.x` remains `>=5.8.0 <5.9.0` (https://angular.dev/reference/versions — table also visible in search result for that page). The Angular repo itself builds with `typescript: 5.9.2` (https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/20.2.x/package.json). The PR itself is purely a support-range expansion: "Updates the repo to support TypeScript 5.9 and expands the allowed version range." (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/62541, https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/745ea4439465494ab5b7002dd1fa320cd32220fb). Per Angular's release policy, minor releases only expand peer dependencies and do not require projects to update them ().

**Affects:** Applications on 20.1 that want to install TypeScript 5.9.x — previously npm/yarn emitted peer-dependency warnings and `ng build` version checks rejected TS 5.9; only possible with Angular ≥ 20.2. No code changes for applications staying on TypeScript 5.8.x.

**Action:** none — optionally upgrade `typescript` to `~5.9.0` in `package.json` after moving to Angular 20.2; re-verify that `ng build` type-checking passes with TS 5.9 (including new TS 5.9 diagnostics in templates/lib code), but no migration step is mandated.

**Before/after:**
```jsonc
// @angular/compiler-cli peerDependencies
// Angular 20.1 (old): "typescript": ">=5.8 <5.9"
// Angular 20.2 (new): "typescript": ">=5.8 <6.0"
```

**What changed:** The `areAnimationSupported` constant in `packages/core/src/render3/instructions/animation.ts` was updated to prevent crashes when the `document` object exists but `document.body` is undefined (e.g., during initial SSR bootstrapping or in specific test environments). Previously, the code accessed `document.body.getAnimations`, which threw an error if `body` was missing (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/62768).
**Affects:** Applications using Angular's animation system (including `animate.enter`, `animate.leave`, or `@angular/animations`) that run in environments where `document.body` might not be immediately available.
**Action:** none: The fix prevents errors; applications do not need to change their code to benefit from this stability improvement.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
const areAnimationSupported =
  (typeof ngServerMode === 'undefined' || !ngServerMode) &&
  typeof document !== 'undefined' &&
  // tslint:disable-next-line:no-toplevel-property-access
  typeof document.body !== 'undefined' &&
  // tslint:disable-next-line:no-toplevel-property-access
  typeof document.body.getAnimations === 'function';
```

**What changed:** Before this fix, when an element had `animate.enter` defined both on the element itself and via a host binding, Angular's internal `animationEnd` function did not have access to all the classes added by both sources. It could only track and remove the last set of classes applied, causing other `animate.enter` classes to remain on the DOM element permanently after the longest animation ended. This commit introduces an internal `WeakMap<HTMLElement, {classList: string[]; cleanupFns: Function[]}>` named `enterClassMap` in the `animation.ts` instruction. This map tracks all classes added by any `animate.enter` call on a specific element instance, ensuring `animationEnd` properly iterates and removes all classes when the animation completes, followed by cleanup of the WeakMap reference. (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/de3a0c5cf3f87782fa63d30edf6ac05eb6be9fac, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/62981)
**Affects:** All applications utilizing the `animate.enter` feature and combining it on the same DOM element with host bindings (e.g., `animate.enter="classA"` directly on an element where a directive or component uses `host: {'[animate.enter]': 'classB'}`). (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/de3a0c5cf3f87782fa63d30edf6ac05eb6be9fac, https://angular.dev/guide/animations)
**Action:** none
**Before/after:**
```html
<!-- Example of composition triggering the issue -->
<!-- Parent Component Template -->
<child-cmp animate.enter="fade-in" />

<!-- Child Component Definition -->
@Component({
  selector: 'child-cmp',
  host: {'[animate.enter]': 'fadeExp'},
  template: '<p>Content</p>'
})
class ChildComponent {
  fadeExp = 'slide-in';
}
```

**What changed:** A bug fix in the `animate.enter` / `animate.leave` (and `(animate.enter)` / `(animate.leave)`) animation system in `@angular/core`. Previously the animation code determined the "longest animation" for an element only via the DOM API `element.getAnimations()`. When something on the page triggered a style recalculation (e.g. a reflow), `getAnimations()` returned an empty array, so Angular could never identify the longest animation: enter classes were never removed and `animate.leave` elements were never removed from the DOM (#63006) (https://github.com/angular/angular/issues/63006). The fix adds `packages/core/src/animation/longest_animation.ts` with `determineLongestAnimation(...)`, which falls back to parsing `getComputedStyle` values (`animation-name`, `animation-delay`, `animation-duration`, `transition-property`, `transition-duration`, `transition-delay`) when `getAnimations()` is empty (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/ebd622b3449789b72efc8295244ca924a299e7c1). The longest-animation state is now stored in a per-element `WeakMap<HTMLElement, LongestAnimation>` (`longestAnimations`) instead of being recomputed per event, longest-animation work only runs when `event.target === el` (bubbled-up animation events from children are now ignored), matching end events call `event.stopImmediatePropagation()`, and enter cleanup functions are tracked so cancelled/removed elements no longer leak `animationend`/`transitionend` listeners. Public symbols (`AnimationCallbackEvent`, `AnimationFunction`, `MAX_ANIMATION_TIMEOUT`, `ɵANIMATIONS_DISABLED`, `ɵElementRegistry`, `ɵAnimationRemovalRegistry`) were only moved internally from `packages/core/src/animation.ts` to `animation/interfaces.ts` and `animation/element_removal_registry.ts` — exports are unchanged (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/63007).

**Affects:** Applications using `animate.enter`, `animate.leave`, `(animate.enter)`, or `(animate.leave)` bindings where CSS animations/transitions are applied and some other code triggers style recalculations/reflows during the animation; previously this caused enter classes to stick or leave elements to never be removed.

**Action:** none — this is an internal bug fix; no code, configuration, or dependency changes are required. Applications that previously worked around the bug (e.g. manually removing enter classes or forcing element removal when `animate.leave` never completed) should re-verify that enter/leave animations now complete on their own.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// old (getAnimations-only, returned undefined when styles were recalculating)
function getLongestAnimation(
  event: AnimationEvent | TransitionEvent,
): LongestAnimation | undefined {
  if (!areAnimationSupported || !(event.target instanceof Element)) return;
  const nativeElement = event.target;
  const animations = nativeElement.getAnimations();
  if (animations.length === 0) return;
  ...
}

// new (falls back to computed styles, only for the element in question)
export function determineLongestAnimation(
  event: AnimationEvent | TransitionEvent,
  el: HTMLElement,
  animationsMap: WeakMap<HTMLElement, LongestAnimation>,
  areAnimationSupported: boolean,
): void {
  if (!areAnimationSupported || !(event.target instanceof Element) || event.target !== el) return;
  const animations = el.getAnimations();
  return animations.length === 0
    ? determineLongestAnimationFromComputedStyles(el, animationsMap)
    : determineLongestAnimationFromElementAnimations(el, animationsMap, animations);
}
```

**What changed:** For `animate.enter` (the `ɵɵanimateEnter` instruction), the check for the longest animation (`getLongestAnimation` / `isLongestAnimation`) was moved from the `animationstart`/`transitionstart` handler (`handleAnimationStart`) to the end handler (`animationEnd`) (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/455b147488dc0a064c0ca13a96a4df3c3ed01152). Previously the longest animation was computed at start via `element.getAnimations()`; in some patterns that returned an empty array even though the animation class was applied and the stylesheet showed a keyframe animation, so the enter class was never removed after the animation completed (https://github.com/angular/angular/issues/62923). Computing the longest animation at the end resolves the case, fixing #62923. The PR is marked non-breaking (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/62925).

**Affects:** Applications using the built-in `animate.enter` (class-based enter animations) where the enter class was failing to be removed after the animation finished because `element.getAnimations()` was empty at animation start.

**Action:** none — internal timing fix; re-verify that `animate.enter` classes are removed after animations complete on affected elements.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// handleInAnimationEnd -> animationEnd call (packages/core/src/render3/instructions/animation.ts)
// before
animationEnd(event, nativeElement, longestAnimation, activeClasses, renderer, cleanupFns);
// after
animationEnd(event, nativeElement, activeClasses, renderer, cleanupFns);
```

**What changed:** The `animations` field on the component interface (used in the `@Component` decorator) is deprecated and replaced by the template attributes `animate.enter` and `animate.leave` (`@angular/animations` is deprecated) (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/62895, https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/320de4e96d250cad1ce2c9f8c0fa2022da53b734). Legacy animations and the new syntax cannot be used within the same component or in projected content ().

**Affects:** Components that declare the `animations` field (e.g. `animations: [...]`) using `@angular/animations`.

**Action:** required: remove the `animations` field from affected component decorators and re-implement enter/leave animations using the `animate.enter` / `animate.leave` template attributes, which are mutually exclusive with legacy animations within the same component ().

**Before/after:**
```
@Component({
  animations: [trigger('fade', [transition(':enter', animate(300, style({opacity: 1})))]),
})
```
```
@if (display()) {
  <div animate.enter="fade-in" animate.leave="fade-out">Content</div>
}
```

**What changed:** `FormArray.push()` signature updated from `push(control: TControl, ...)` to `push(control: TControl | Array<TControl>, ...)` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/c353497a01776cd702af6c5136fdae5fc6ce94d5). Previously, users had to iterate through an array and push each `FormControl` individually, which triggered multiple `valueChanges` and `statusChanges` events and multiple validation passes. Now, passing an array to `push()` registers all controls at once but emits only a single event after the addition is complete, improving performance for large datasets (https://angular.love/angular-20-2-the-recent-changes, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/57102).
**Affects:** Applications that dynamically add multiple controls to a `FormArray` using `push()` inside a loop. Existing code passing a single control to `push()` is unaffected and backwards-compatible.
**Action:** required: Update logic that loops over an array to push controls to a `FormArray`. Replace the `forEach` loop with a single call to `push()` passing the array. Re-verify event subscription behavior, as `valueChanges` and `statusChanges` will now emit once for the batch operation instead of once per control.
**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Before Angular 20.2:
this._controls.forEach((control) => {
  this.formArray.push(control);
});

// After Angular 20.2:
this.formArray.push(this._controls);
```

**What changed:** Added optional `referrer?: string` and `integrity?: string` properties to the `HttpResourceRequest` interface. When using `httpResource` with the `withFetch` provider, these values are passed through to the underlying `Request` constructor, enabling control over the request's referrer policy and Subresource Integrity (SRI) validation (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/be811fee7925fb482567fa7cd9d485ac28acdade, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/62461).

**Affects:** Applications using `httpResource` in conjunction with `provideHttpClient(withFetch())` that require or want to specify the `referrer` or `integrity` request options.

**Action:** none

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Before
httpResource(() => ({
  url: '/assets/data.json',
  method: 'GET'
}));

// After
httpResource(() => ({
  url: '/assets/data.json',
  method: 'GET',
  referrer: 'no-referrer',
  integrity: 'sha384-oqVuAfXRKap7fdgcCY5uykM6+R9GhEXAMPLEKEY='
}));
```

**What changed:** Angular 20.2.0 adds the Fetch API `referrer` and `integrity` options to `HttpClient`. `referrer?: string` and `integrity?: string` were added to the `HttpRequestInit` interface and to all `HttpRequest` constructor option overloads in `packages/common/http/src/request.ts`, exposed as readonly `HttpRequest.referrer` / `HttpRequest.integrity` properties (the commit copy states values are only assigned when truthy: `if (options.integrity) {...} if (options.referrer) {...}`) (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/1cf9d9064c15c00071ece3b78c8019035a6db6ce, request.ts at that commit). `FetchBackend.createRequestInit` now forwards them to native `fetch` alongside existing `keepalive`, `cache`, `priority`, `mode`, `redirect`, `referrerPolicy` (`referrer: req.referrer, integrity: req.integrity`) (https://github.com/angular/angular/blob/main/packages/common/http/src/fetch.ts). Per the PR, `referrer` customizes/restricts the Referer info (`'no-referrer'`, `'origin'`, or a URL) and `integrity` enables Subresource Integrity hash verification (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/62417). Docs note the integrity mismatch causes a network error, and under SSR the Fetch implementation buffers the whole body for integrity checks before `maxResponseBodySize` applies (https://angular.dev/guide/http/making-requests).

**Affects:** Only applications that opt in to using these new per-request options; no existing behavior changes — the options are ignored by the XHR backend and only take effect with the fetch backend.

**Action:** none (purely additive opt-in feature; no code, config, or dependency changes required on upgrade).

**Before/after:**
```ts
// New usage (Angular 20.2+, fetch backend), from PR #62417:
// Request without referrer header and with integrity check
http.get('/scripts/app.js', { referrer: 'no-referrer', integrity: 'sha512-...' }).subscribe();
```

**What changed:** A language-service (Angular extension for VS Code) fix now resolves components that are re-exported through `export {X as Y} from ...` chains. Previously the Angular language service matched completion/import entries by exact symbol name and file, so when a directive was imported via a re-exported alias (a distinct TypeScript symbol but the same Angular component/selector), the "add import to NgModule / add to `imports`" code fix and completions could fail to resolve the module specifier. The fix propagates alias information through the internal `TemplateTypeChecker` completion-entry cache (`appendOrReplaceTsEntryInfo`, `isSymbolAliasOf`, `findTsCompletionEntryInfoForImport`) and changes the internal API `PotentialDirectiveModuleSpecifierResolver.resolve` to return `DirectiveModuleExportDetails {moduleSpecifier, exportName}` instead of `string | undefined`, so the language service emits the import using the most recent re-export name and module specifier, including tsconfig `paths` aliases such as `@app/*` (e.g. imports `NewBarComponent3` from `"@app/index"`) (<https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/591c7e2ec82c6669ffa6e0011b8a0a4fc12e9c3a>, <https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/62585>).

**Affects:** Applications where standalone components use directives/components that are consumed through re-exported alias names (e.g. barrel files with `export {Foo as Bar} from ...`); purely an editor/language-service behavior change — no runtime, compiler, or build output change. The PR explicitly states it is not a breaking change.

**Action:** none — no application code, configuration, or dependency changes are needed; only the Angular language service's completions and code fixes for re-exported components change behavior.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// component/share/index.ts (re-export chain that previously broke LS import fixes)
export {NewBarComponent2 as NewBarComponent3} from "./public_api"
// With 20.2, the LS code fix now generates:
import { NewBarComponent3 } from "@app/index";
```

**What changed:** Angular 20.2.0 adds an internal injection token to detect when both `provideClientHydration()` (from `@angular/platform-browser`) and `withEnabledBlockingInitialNavigation()` (from `@angular/router`) are enabled in the same application. During app bootstrap, a `console.warn` is now logged if this unsupported combination is found (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/62963). According to the Angular documentation, `withEnabledBlockingInitialNavigation()` "should be set in case you use server-side rendering, but do not enable hydration for your application" (https://angular.dev/api/router/withEnabledBlockingInitialNavigation).

**Affects:** All applications that use `@angular/platform-browser` and `@angular/router`, specifically those configuring both `provideClientHydration()` and `withEnabledBlockingInitialNavigation()` in their providers.

**Action:** required: Re-verify your application configuration. If you are using SSR with hydration (`provideClientHydration()`), remove `withEnabledBlockingInitialNavigation()` from your `provideRouter` configuration to prevent the console warning and ensure stable application performance. `withEnabledBlockingInitialNavigation()` should be used only for SSR apps without standard hydration to prevent UI flickers.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// app.config.ts
import { provideRouter, withEnabledBlockingInitialNavigation } from '@angular/router';
import { provideClientHydration } from '@angular/platform-browser';

export const appConfig: ApplicationConfig = {
  providers: [
    // Affects: This combination will now log a warning during bootstrap
    provideClientHydration(),
    provideRouter(routes, withEnabledBlockingInitialNavigation())
  ]
};
```

**What changed:** Angular 20.2 added a new `currentNavigation` signal to the `Router` service. The API docs describe it as: "Signal of the current Navigation object when the router is navigating, and null when idle. Note: The current navigation becomes to null after the NavigationEnd event is emitted." (https://v20.angular.dev/api/router/Router). The existing `Router.getCurrentNavigation` method is now deprecated: "Returns the current Navigation object when the router is navigating, and null when idle. Use the currentNavigation signal instead." (https://v20.angular.dev/api/router/Router). The new signal was introduced because `getCurrentNavigation()` was reported to return a non-null `Navigation` object even during the `NavigationEnd` event (https://github.com/angular/angular/issues/62958), while the signal returns null after `NavigationEnd` (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/62971). It is meant to derive an `isNavigating` state: `isNavigating = computed(() => !!this.router.currentNavigation())` (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/62971).

**Affects:** Applications that call `Router.getCurrentNavigation()` (e.g. to detect an in-progress navigation); `@angular/router`.

**Action:** required: replace `router.getCurrentNavigation()` with the `router.currentNavigation()` signal. A migration is provided that runs automatically with `ng update` (https://blog.ninja-squad.com/2025/08/20/what-is-new-angular-20.2). Re-verify navigation-feedback logic that relied on `getCurrentNavigation()` returning a `Navigation` object after `NavigationEnd`.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
export class App {
  private router = inject(Router);

  // New approach - simple and reactive
  isNavigating = computed(() => !!this.router.currentNavigation());
}
```

**What changed:** Angular 20.2 adds a new read-only signal property `currentNavigation` to the `Router` service in `@angular/router`: `readonly currentNavigation = this.navigationTransitions.currentNavigation.asReadonly();` — "Signal of the current `Navigation` object when the router is navigating, and `null` when idle. Note: The current navigation becomes to null after the NavigationEnd event is emitted." (). The existing method `Router.getCurrentNavigation(): Navigation | null` is deprecated in 20.2 with the tag `@deprecated 20.2 Use the currentNavigation signal instead.` (same file). The signal is the fix for issue #62958, where `getCurrentNavigation()` could still return a non-null `Navigation` object after `NavigationEnd`; the signal reliably becomes `null` when navigation completes, making it easy to derive state such as `isNavigating = computed(() => !!this.router.currentNavigation())` (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/63011, https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/687c374826c5e9ea91839c20f0df815ce085c583). The commit is labeled "action: merge, area: router, detected: feature, detected: deprecation" (PR page).

**Affects:** Applications that call `router.getCurrentNavigation()` (for example in guards, resolvers, or `Router.events` subscriptions for navigation feedback).

**Action:** required: replace calls to `Router.getCurrentNavigation()` with the `Router.currentNavigation` signal (call it as a signal, e.g. inside `computed`/`effect` or in a template). Verify code that read `getCurrentNavigation()` inside a `NavigationEnd` event handler — the new signal is `null` at that point, which is the intended fix but changes behavior for code that relied on the stale non-null value.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// Before
getCurrentNavigation(): Navigation | null {
  return untracked(this.navigationTransitions.currentNavigation);
}

// After (Angular 20.2)
readonly currentNavigation = this.navigationTransitions.currentNavigation.asReadonly();
isNavigating = computed(() => !!this.router.currentNavigation());
```

